--TEST--
a `Core\Attributes` retrieval reads its target and its written member name syntactically, so a target that is only a `callable` value and a member name naming no declaration are both refused where they are written, and `get` refuses a declaration with two payloads on its target (`rule:attributes/a-retrieval-matches-the-declaration-it-names` and `rule:attributes/retrieval-folds-while-checking`)
--FILE--
<?nvs
// covers: lang:attributes/reading-attributes-back-core-attributes-get-and-all
// `rule:attributes/a-retrieval-matches-the-declaration-it-names`: the target is read where it is written and
// never evaluated, and a *written* member name is checked against the target's
// real declarations, because the empty result a misspelling folds to is the
// one a correct retrieval of an absent attribute gives and nothing later can
// tell the two apart. A computed `$member` has no name to check and is the one
// spelling that does fall back to that empty result.
repeatable attribute Tag on class, property, parameter { name: string }

#[Tag(name: "class-a")]
#[Tag(name: "class-b")]
class Target {
    #[Tag(name: "prop-a")]
    public int $rows = 0;

    public function run(#[Tag(name: "param-a")] int $id): int {
        return $id;
    }
}

// The accepted half first, so a spelling that stops being accepted fails here
// rather than as a missing refusal. Every target below is written out, every
// member name names a real declaration, and the class's two `Tag` payloads
// are read with `all`, which is what `get`'s refusal names as the fix.
var $both = Core\Attributes::all<Tag>(Target::constructor);
var $property = Core\Attributes::get<Tag>(Target::constructor, "rows");
var $parameter = Core\Attributes::get<Tag>(Target::run, "id");

// A `callable` variable is a value, and the reference it was built from is not
// written here, so there is nothing at this call to read a declaration out of.
callable $reference = (new Target())->run(...);
var $computedTarget = Core\Attributes::get<Tag>($reference);

// `rows` written one letter wrong. The class declares no `$row`, and folding
// this to the empty result would make the misspelling look like an absent
// attribute for the rest of the program's life.
var $misspelled = Core\Attributes::get<Tag>(Target::constructor, "row");

// A parameter name asked of the class rather than of the method that declares
// it. Neither of the class's two rosters names `id`.
var $wrongRoster = Core\Attributes::get<Tag>(Target::constructor, "id");

// The class has two `Tag` payloads, and `get` returns at most one.
var $ambiguous = Core\Attributes::get<Tag>(Target::constructor);

echo "unreachable\n";
--EXPECT--

--EXPECTF-ERROR--
error[E0730]: a `Core\Attributes` retrieval names no declaration
   --> case.nvs:33:49
%A
error[E0798]: a `Core\Attributes` retrieval names `$row`, which `Target` does not declare
   --> case.nvs:38:66
%A
error[E0798]: a `Core\Attributes` retrieval names `$id`, which `Target` does not declare
   --> case.nvs:42:67
%A
error[E0728]: `Target::constructor` has 2 `Tag` attributes
   --> case.nvs:45:18
%A
